== (2) certification thresholds N(w)/2^A of first-descent classes, max over words with j <= 14, by (j, A) ==
 180510 classes examined; the largest N(w)/2^A per (j, A) (only pairs with ratio > 0.05 shown):
  j= 1 A= 2  2^A/3^j=1.33333  N(w)=1.0  N/2^A=0.2500  worst word (2,)
  j= 3 A= 5  2^A/3^j=1.18519  N(w)=4.6  N/2^A=0.1437  worst word (1, 2, 2)
  j= 5 A= 8  2^A/3^j=1.05350  N(w)=24.5  N/2^A=0.0959  worst word (1, 2, 1, 2, 2)
 the largest thresholds sit at the near-convergent pairs (j, A) = (5, 8), (12, 19) [2^19 < 3^12: no descent], (41, 65) ...
 bound: N(w)/2^A <= ((3/2)^j - 1)/(2^A - 3^j), since S_j <= 2^A ((3/2)^j - 1); every class has at most one uncertified member (its representative rho_w) once N(w) < 2^A.
== (2b) actual exceptions: first-descent classes (j <= 14) whose representative rho_w <= N(w) ==
 606746 classes checked (all v up to 40 beyond the critical valuation); exceptions (rho, word, N(w), U^j(rho)):
   (1, (2,), 1.0, 1)
 every other class is certified in full by its coefficient descent; for the exceptions the actual j-th iterate is listed (it is >= rho, so these are the only members of their classes needing a direct check).
== (3) trivial window-avoidance bound ==
 odd n whose first L Syracuse iterates all avoid the k-step certified region (k >= 1) must have v = 1 at each of them: density exactly 2^-L for k = 1; for k >= 2 at most that.
